    <DataNode elementId="AT1G42970-MONOMER" textLabel="Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase GAPB, chloroplastic" type="Protein" groupRef="CPLXQT-49">
      <Xref identifier="AT1G42970-MONOMER" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="195.0" centerY="675" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">It is unclear whether this enzyme is capable of functioning as a monomer. In higher plant chloroplasts, the GapB enzyme is part of an A2B2 heterotetramer (see |FRAME: CPLXQT-49| and |FRAME: CPLXQT-48|). Interestingly, the GapB protein is very similar to the GapA proteins in its N-terminal end, but, it has a C-terminal extension (CTE) with homology to a portion of the CP12 negative regulatory protein (see |FRAME: AT3G62410|). While |FRAME: AT3G62410|) makes the A4-GADPH complex redox-sensitive, the CTE of GapB makes the A2B2 complex redox-sensitive and subject to autoregulation. Both of these mechanisms help to reduce the NADPH-dependent activity of the enzyme complexes in the dark |CITS: [17031544]|. GapB has not yet been experimentally characterized in Arabidopsis, but its activity and participation in complexes has been inferred from other studies in the chloroplasts of higher plants |CITS: [17031544]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT1G42970-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="Synonym_1" value="NADP-dependent glyceraldehydephosphate dehydrogenase B" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT1G42970.1" />
      <Property key="ComponentOf" value="CPLXQT-49, CPLXQT-48" />
      <Property key="FunctionalAssignmentStatus" value=":AMBIGUOUS" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="AT1G42970" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="MONOMER-*" />
    </DataNode>
